{"verify_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_91"],"intended_action":"simplify_onboarding","scale":"standard_experiment","uses_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_31","memory_91","memory_73"],"downstream_value_risk_flagged":true,"preserves_uncertainty":true,"rationale":"The competitor's entry pricing creates pressure to match with promotional_pricing, but memory_73 is the one inherited record that actually traces a growth tactic through to downstream value, and it is unfavorable: +31% new accounts and +18% booked revenue bought at the cost of -12pp first-term renewals among price-sensitive buyers. That is precisely the buyer segment the competitor is taking, so a promo would concentrate acquisition in the cohort with the worst demonstrated retention — booked revenue up, sustainable qualified revenue likely down. I am declining that direction rather than spending verification on it.

The two observed declines (onboarding completion -4%, activation -6%) point at memory_86/memory_31 (onboarding and signup simplification) and memory_91 (in-product activation messaging). I am spending both verification pulls there because those are the memories I intend to act on, and because their summaries share a specific weakness: every reported outcome is a proxy — completion rate, time-to-value, week-one engagement, 'first meaningful action.' None is stated in terms of retained or qualified revenue. memory_57 by contrast explicitly checked retention and found no harm, which shows the archive is capable of recording that check; its absence in 86/91 is informative. So I want the source records for memory_86 and memory_91 to see (a) whether the lifts were ever followed through to revenue or renewal, (b) the baseline cohort and whether it resembles today's mix, and (c) whether the onboarding shortening removed steps that were doing qualifying work — a shorter funnel can raise completion while lowering the quality of who completes, which would show up later as exactly the activation decline we are now seeing. It is worth holding open that the current 4-6% declines may be partly a downstream consequence of prior simplification wins rather than an independent problem for more simplification to solve.

I intend simplify_onboarding at standard experiment scale: it is supported by two independent inherited records (memory_31, memory_86), it plausibly addresses both declining metrics since onboarding feeds activation, and it does not buy volume by degrading cohort quality the way a promo would. I am not going broad — the supporting evidence is proxy-only and possibly stale at day 12/52. Instrumentation must carry revenue-linked and renewal-linked endpoints, not completion rate alone, so this run repairs the measurement gap in the memories it relies on. If verification shows memory_86's lift never touched revenue or came from a materially different cohort, I would drop to a small guarded test or switch to activation_messaging as the more direct fit to the larger (-6%) decline. enterprise_sales_assist (memory_44) is real but aimed at a segment that is not the one under attack, and it does not address either declining metric this week.","confidence":66}
